Task 1: Understanding Ansible Playbook Structure
Subtask 1.1: Explore the Lab Environment

First, let's familiarize ourselves with the environment and verify Ansible installation.

    Connect to the control node and verify Ansible installation:

ansible --version

Expected output should show Ansible version 2.9 or higher.

    Check the inventory file to see available managed nodes:

cat /etc/ansible/hosts

    Test connectivity to managed nodes:

ansible all -m ping

Subtask 1.2: Examine Playbook Components

Create a working directory for your playbooks:

mkdir ~/ansible-lab
cd ~/ansible-lab

Let's understand the basic structure of an Ansible playbook by examining each component:

Playbook Structure Overview:

    Hosts: Defines target machines
    Tasks: Specific actions to perform
    Modules: Ansible components that perform actual work
    Variables: Dynamic values used throughout playbooks
    Handlers: Tasks triggered by other tasks

Task 2: Create a Simple Package Installation Playbook
Subtask 2.1: Create the Basic Playbook Structure

Create your first playbook to install the htop package on managed nodes.

    Create a new playbook file:

nano install-package.yml

    Add the following content to create a basic playbook structure:

---
- name: Install htop package on managed nodes
  hosts: all
  become: yes
  become_method: sudo
  
  tasks:
    - name: Update package cache (Ubuntu/Debian)
      apt:
        update_cache: yes
        cache_valid_time: 3600
      when: ansible_os_family == "Debian"
      
    - name: Update package cache (CentOS/RHEL)
      yum:
        update_cache: yes
      when: ansible_os_family == "RedHat"
      
    - name: Install htop package on Ubuntu/Debian systems
      apt:
        name: htop
        state: present
      when: ansible_os_family == "Debian"
      
    - name: Install htop package on CentOS/RHEL systems
      yum:
        name: htop
        state: present
      when: ansible_os_family == "RedHat"
      
    - name: Verify htop installation
      command: which htop
      register: htop_path
      changed_when: false
      
    - name: Display htop installation path
      debug:
        msg: "htop is installed at: {{ htop_path.stdout }}"

    Save and exit the file (Ctrl+X, then Y, then Enter in nano).

Subtask 2.2: Understanding Playbook Components

Let's break down each section of our playbook:

Header Section:

    ---: YAML document start marker
    name: Human-readable description of the playbook
    hosts: Specifies target machines (all means all hosts in inventory)
    become: Enables privilege escalation (sudo)

Tasks Section:

    Each task has a descriptive name
    Uses specific modules (apt, yum, command, debug)
    Includes conditional statements with when
    Uses variables like ansible_os_family

Subtask 2.3: Create an Enhanced Playbook with Variables

Create a more advanced version using variables:

nano install-package-advanced.yml

Add the following content:

---
- name: Install packages using variables and loops
  hosts: all
  become: yes
  vars:
    packages_to_install:
      - htop
      - curl
      - wget
      - tree
    
  tasks:
    - name: Update package cache (Ubuntu/Debian)
      apt:
        update_cache: yes
        cache_valid_time: 3600
      when: ansible_os_family == "Debian"
      
    - name: Update package cache (CentOS/RHEL)
      yum:
        update_cache: yes
      when: ansible_os_family == "RedHat"
      
    - name: Install packages on Ubuntu/Debian systems
      apt:
        name: "{{ item }}"
        state: present
      loop: "{{ packages_to_install }}"
      when: ansible_os_family == "Debian"
      
    - name: Install packages on CentOS/RHEL systems
      yum:
        name: "{{ item }}"
        state: present
      loop: "{{ packages_to_install }}"
      when: ansible_os_family == "RedHat"
      
    - name: Create a simple report file
      copy:
        content: |
          Package Installation Report
          ==========================
          Date: {{ ansible_date_time.date }}
          Time: {{ ansible_date_time.time }}
          Host: {{ ansible_hostname }}
          OS Family: {{ ansible_os_family }}
          Packages Installed: {{ packages_to_install | join(', ') }}
        dest: /tmp/installation_report.txt
        mode: '0644'
      
    - name: Display installation summary
      debug:
        msg: "Successfully installed {{ packages_to_install | length }} packages on {{ ansible_hostname }}"

Task 3: Run the Playbook and Verify Execution
Subtask 3.1: Execute the Basic Playbook

    Run the first playbook with verbose output:

ansible-playbook -v install-package.yml

    Analyze the output and look for:
        PLAY RECAP: Shows success/failure status for each host
        changed: Number of tasks that made changes
        ok: Number of tasks that completed successfully
        unreachable: Number of hosts that couldn't be reached
        failed: Number of failed tasks

Subtask 3.2: Execute the Advanced Playbook

    Run the advanced playbook with increased verbosity:

ansible-playbook -vv install-package-advanced.yml

    Monitor the execution and observe:
        Variable substitution in action
        Loop execution for multiple packages
        Conditional task execution based on OS family

Subtask 3.3: Verify Installation Success

    Check installed packages on managed nodes:

ansible all -m command -a "htop --version"

    Verify the report file was created:

ansible all -m command -a "cat /tmp/installation_report.txt"

    List all installed packages from our playbook:

ansible all -m shell -a "dpkg -l | grep -E 'htop|curl|wget|tree'" --become

Task 4: Playbook Execution Verification and Troubleshooting
Subtask 4.1: Implement Dry Run Testing

Before making changes, always test with dry run mode:

    Execute a dry run to see what would happen:

ansible-playbook --check install-package-advanced.yml

    Compare dry run output with actual execution results.

Subtask 4.2: Create a Verification Playbook

Create a separate playbook to verify installations:

nano verify-installation.yml

Add the following content:

---
- name: Verify package installations
  hosts: all
  become: yes
  vars:
    expected_packages:
      - htop
      - curl
      - wget
      - tree
      
  tasks:
    - name: Check if packages are installed (Ubuntu/Debian)
      command: dpkg -l {{ item }}
      register: package_check_deb
      loop: "{{ expected_packages }}"
      when: ansible_os_family == "Debian"
      failed_when: package_check_deb.rc != 0
      changed_when: false
      
    - name: Check if packages are installed (CentOS/RHEL)
      command: rpm -q {{ item }}
      register: package_check_rpm
      loop: "{{ expected_packages }}"
      when: ansible_os_family == "RedHat"
      failed_when: package_check_rpm.rc != 0
      changed_when: false
      
    - name: Verify htop functionality
      command: htop --version
      register: htop_version
      changed_when: false
      
    - name: Display verification results
      debug:
        msg: "All packages verified successfully. htop version: {{ htop_version.stdout }}"

    Run the verification playbook:

ansible-playbook verify-installation.yml

Subtask 4.3: Common Troubleshooting Scenarios

Issue 1: SSH Connection Problems

If you encounter SSH connectivity issues:

# Test SSH connectivity manually
ssh -i ~/.ssh/id_rsa user@managed_node_ip

# Check SSH key permissions
ls -la ~/.ssh/
chmod 600 ~/.ssh/id_rsa

Issue 2: Permission Denied Errors

If you get permission errors:

# Verify sudo access on managed nodes
ansible all -m command -a "sudo whoami"

# Check if become is properly configured
ansible-playbook install-package.yml --ask-become-pass

Issue 3: Package Not Found Errors

If packages aren't found:

# Update package cache manually
ansible all -m apt -a "update_cache=yes" --become
ansible all -m yum -a "update_cache=yes" --become

Advanced Concepts and Best Practices
Playbook Organization

Create a more structured approach:

mkdir -p ~/ansible-lab/group_vars
mkdir -p ~/ansible-lab/host_vars
mkdir -p ~/ansible-lab/roles

Using Tags for Selective Execution

Modify your playbook to include tags:

- name: Install packages on Ubuntu/Debian systems
  apt:
    name: "{{ item }}"
    state: present
  loop: "{{ packages_to_install }}"
  when: ansible_os_family == "Debian"
  tags:
    - packages
    - debian

Run specific tagged tasks:

ansible-playbook install-package-advanced.yml --tags "packages"

Error Handling and Recovery

Add error handling to your playbooks:

- name: Install package with error handling
  apt:
    name: "{{ item }}"
    state: present
  loop: "{{ packages_to_install }}"
  register: install_result
  ignore_errors: yes
  
- name: Report failed installations
  debug:
    msg: "Failed to install: {{ item.item }}"
  loop: "{{ install_result.results }}"
  when: item.failed is defined and item.failed

